        "markdown": "This is the blameless review for the checkout outage on Friday, March 14."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n3",
      "type": "heading",
      "content": {
        "text": "Timeline of Outage"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n4",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "At 16:40 a config change to the payments service went out. It changed the gateway connection pool from 50 connections to 5. This was a typo; the intent was 55. Checkout requests started queuing for a connection and timing out almost immediately."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n5",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Our error-rate alert fired at 17:02, 22 minutes after the change. The config was rolled back at 17:15, and checkout was fully recovered at 17:27. Customers saw errors for 47 minutes in total."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n6",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "About 2,300 failed checkouts occurred in that window. Support received 140 tickets before the status page was posted."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n7",
      "type": "heading",
      "content": {
        "text": "Alerting Issues"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n8",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The alert only fires when the checkout error rate is above 10% for 15 minutes. Errors went over 10% around 16:47, so the earliest it could fire was 17:02. It did exactly what it was configured to do, but this is too slow for checkout."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n9",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Proposed change: 5% over 5 minutes for checkout specifically. This would have paged twice on noise in the last 30 days, both during the payment provider's own maintenance. Two noisy pages a month is fine for checkout."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n10",
      "type": "todo",
      "content": {
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Lower the checkout error-rate alert to 5% over 5 minutes (Marcus to own, by Wednesday, March 19).",
            "checked": false
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n11",
      "type": "heading",
      "content": {
        "text": "Status Page Update Process"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n12",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The status page went up at 17:20, 40 minutes after the errors started. Customers were asking on social media before an update was provided."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n13",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Currently, the incident commander posts the status page. On Friday, Marcus was incident commander and was busy rolling back, so it was the last thing on his mind."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n14",
      "type": "todo",
      "content": {
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Allow support to post a status-page update directly, without waiting for the incident commander.",
            "checked": false
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n15",
      "type": "heading",
      "content": {
        "text": "Config Change Process"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n16",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Config changes don't go through code review. They are edited in the deploy dashboard and applied directly. Code changes need two approvals; config needs none. This is a gap, as a typo in code would have been caught by a reviewer or a test."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n17",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Proposal: payments config goes through the same review as code."
      }
    }
